README.md:5:Curator is implemented in Go and follows the [Curator Specification](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec), an open protocol for skill packages, project manifests, installation semantics, and the audit registry; sections are cited across this repository as `Spec §N.M`.
README.md:9:v0.1 development complete: all twelve phases of [docs/implementation-plan.md](docs/implementation-plan.md) are done. CI consumes the authoritative schemas and conformance vectors from `curator-spec` on ubuntu, macos, and windows, plus lint and the naming gate. Work is tracked on the in-repo task board under [.task-board/](.task-board/).
README.md:36:- **Skill packages**: `SKILL.md` plus context directories, with a machine manifest (`csk-skill.json`, schemas 1 through 5) declaring commands, runtime layout, capabilities, and dependencies.
README.md:47:built from it interoperates with the same skills, the same project manifests,
README.md:52:conformance against the shared wire formats is enforced directly from the
docs/implementation-plan.md:3:This is the working plan for building Curator, an agent environment manager (AEM) in Go. Iterations consult this document and the task board; the plan is updated as decisions land. Protocol sections are cited as `Spec §N.M` against the 1.0.0-draft [Curator Specification](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec).
docs/implementation-plan.md:7:Curator v0.1 is a conforming independent implementation of the protocol: it works with the same skill packages, the same project manifests, the same installed layouts, and the same audit registries as the reference implementation, per the eight conformance requirements of Spec §17.1. Done means:
docs/implementation-plan.md:9:1. All conformance-relevant behavior is implemented and covered by tests, including the golden interoperability suite (Section 6 below).
docs/implementation-plan.md:11:3. Markers, hashes, and runtime layouts produced by Curator are byte-compatible with the golden fixtures.
docs/implementation-plan.md:28:| Hashing | stdlib `crypto/sha256`; content hash per Spec §8.5 byte layout | Byte compatibility is a conformance requirement |
docs/implementation-plan.md:30:| Testing | stdlib `testing`, table-driven; authoritative vectors from `curator-spec/conformance/v1` | Deterministic and shared by independent implementations |
docs/implementation-plan.md:33:| Machine home | `~/.curator/` (env `CURATOR_CONFIG`, `CURATOR_SYSTEM_CONFIG`; system config `/etc/curator/config.json`, `%ProgramData%\curator`) | The machine home is tool-specific state (config, caches, runtime store), not a wire format; two managers on one machine must not share it. Shared wire formats stay exactly as specified: `Skillfile.json`, `csk-skill.json`, markers, adapter ledgers |
docs/implementation-plan.md:41:- The name of the alternative protocol implementation appears exactly once: the README open-protocol section. A CI check greps case-insensitively (zero matches outside README.md, exactly one inside). The protocol is cited as `Spec §N.M` against [curator-spec](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec); protocol file names (`Skillfile.json`, `csk-skill.json`, `.csk-install.json`, `.csk-managed.json`) are part of the wire format and are used as-is.
docs/implementation-plan.md:58:- `internal/skillspec`: `csk-skill.json` schemas 1 through 5 with every rule of Spec §5 (schema gating, unknown-field rejection for v2+, identifier alphabet §5.2, runtime_roots validation §5.3, script/system commands §5.4, capabilities §5.5 with defaults, dependencies.commands §5.6 incl. legacy skill type, dependencies.skills §5.7 incl. range-marker and branch rejection, mcp_servers §5.8, no install hooks §5.9, legacy `agents/runtime.json` fallback §5.10).
docs/implementation-plan.md:73:Acceptance includes hash equality against the authoritative shared vectors.
docs/implementation-plan.md:79:Test strategy: graph fixtures (diamonds, cycles, cross-version, cross-source, narrowing) with exact error assertions.
docs/implementation-plan.md:87:- `internal/adapters`: managed ledger `.csk-managed.json`, symlink/copy/auto, unmanaged-conflict refusal, native-discovery agents (§10).
docs/implementation-plan.md:105:Test strategy: a local httptest registry serving signed fixtures; keys generated in tests; rollback, freeze, tamper, offline scenarios.
docs/implementation-plan.md:115:### Phase 10: interoperability golden suite (EPIC interop-golden-suite)
docs/implementation-plan.md:117:The authoritative suite is owned by `curator-spec/conformance/v1`: skill packages with expected context file lists, marker objects, content hashes, signed registry records, snapshots, logs, and negative cases. CI checks out the released suite and passes its path through `CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT`; Curator keeps orchestration tests but no implementation-owned expected values.
docs/implementation-plan.md:131:- Interop: the external versioned protocol suite (Phase 10) as the conformance gate.
docs/implementation-plan.md:137:- Byte-compatibility of the content hash and marker JSON is the highest-risk surface; build Phase 2 hashing against the shared protocol vectors first.
docs/implementation-plan.md:141:- Scope creep into registry service or TUI before the conformance gate; the board order enforces the gate.
